Generic single-database configuration.

---

Added for ShipSync (see PR that introduced this file for context):

This is NEW infrastructure, ready for the *next* schema change - it does
NOT yet replace how the app actually migrates its schema today. Right now,
app/main.py still runs Base.metadata.create_all() plus a list of manual
`ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS` statements on every startup, and
that is unchanged by this commit. The baseline migration in
alembic/versions/ was generated from - and verified byte-for-byte identical
to (via pg_dump schema diff) - that exact same current schema, so Alembic
starts out perfectly in sync with what's already running in production.

Why add this now if nothing changes yet: growing that manual ALTER TABLE
list in main.py doesn't scale (no down-migrations, no real history, easy to
forget a statement, every startup re-checks every column on every table
forever). Alembic is the standard, safer way to manage schema changes going
forward - this PR only sets up the tooling and proves it matches reality.

How to use it for a FUTURE schema change (once the team decides to actually
switch over - see the open question below):
    1. Change a model in app/models/
    2. Run `alembic revision --autogenerate -m "short description"`
    3. Read the generated file in alembic/versions/ - autogenerate is a
       starting point, not gospel (it won't catch everything, e.g. column
       renames show up as a drop+add unless you edit the migration by hand)
    4. Run `alembic upgrade head` against a local/staging database to test it
    5. Commit the migration file and deploy; run `alembic upgrade head`
       against production as part of the deploy step

Open question this PR deliberately leaves unresolved: whether to now strip
main.py's create_all()/ALTER TABLE block and make `alembic upgrade head` the
only way schema changes happen (the "correct" end state), or run both side
by side for a while. That changes the production deploy process (Render
needs a migration step before/alongside starting the app) and deserves an
explicit decision rather than a silent switch - the app keeps working
exactly as before until that's decided.
